---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { href } from '../lib/notes';

// Edit this list whenever the focus shifts, and bump the date.
const currentlyUpdated = '2026-11-09';
const currently = [
  'Writing up notes on sequence-level distillation losses and forward vs. reverse KL.',
  'Revisiting azeotropes and pressure-swing distillation.',
  'Planning a grain-based wash as the next batch.',
];
---

<Base title="About" description="Whose notebook this is, and why it’s called the 3-way distiller." current="about">
  <article class="entry">
    <h1>About</h1>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        I’m Sungmin, a first-year grad student. This is my notebook, kept in public mostly so that I
        write things down properly.
      </p>
      <p>
        The name comes from three kinds of distillation that have followed me around: training small
        language models to imitate large ones, which I study now; separating mixtures in columns; and
        making spirits at home. All three are the same move. Take something complex and keep only what
        matters. That is also what I try to do in each entry here.
      </p>
    </div>

    <section class="about-block">
      <h2 class="label">Currently · updated <time datetime={currentlyUpdated}>{currentlyUpdated}</time></h2>
      <ul>
        {currently.map((item) => <li>{item}</li>)}
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="about-block about-block-quiet">
      <h2 class="label">How to read this</h2>
      <p>
        Entries marked <span class="rough">rough</span> are unfinished thinking and
        may be wrong. Longer entries open with a short distillate, so you can stop there.
      </p>
    </section>

    <p class="back"><a href={href()}>← index</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>
